from common import *
it('KERNEL-0056','interface','3.2','interface Frame { widthCm: Cm; heightCm: Cm; aspect: number (width/height, 0.5-2); marginShare: 0.3 (context margin per side) }.',['§3.2 code block'],'kernel (field Plat.frame declared in §2.2)',['@frameRule'],['§2.2 Plat.frame','@frameGrowth','§8 VectorBackend frame box','§4.1 S1'],['Frame','marginShare'])
it('KERNEL-0057','rule','3.2','(1) Integer world: all persisted geometry is integer cm; intermediate rational points (crossings, offsets, circumcentres, skeleton nodes) are computed in doubles and immediately placed on the lattice - by the §3.6 placement rule for points on existing edges, by nearest-point rounding otherwise.',['§3.2 (1)'],'kernel',['@placement'],['@snap','@skeleton','@oneEngine','§2.2 all geometry'],['integer cm','nearest-point rounding'])
it('KERNEL-0058','constant','3.2','(1) Range invariant: |x|, |y| < 2^23 cm (83.9 km), enforced by snap (7); then every coordinate difference < 2^24, every orient2d term < 2^48, the determinant < 2^49, so the sign of orient2d is exact in ordinary doubles. Every exactness argument rests on this invariant, never on the frame table, so a rescaled frame (§13.7 O14 (e)) cannot break one.',['§3.2 (1)','§3.2 (2) (range 2^23 of (1))','§3.2 (6)','§3.2 (7)','§3.2 (9)','§2.2 LatticeSpec.rangeCm = 8 388 608'],'kernel',['§2.2 LatticeSpec.rangeCm'],['@orient2d','@area2','@snap','@frameGrowth','§13.7 O14 (e)'],['rangeInvariant','rangeCm','2^23'])
it('KERNEL-0059','constant','3.2','(1) Headroom check: the largest arena is METROPOLIS at the population cap 120 000 (§12.2: 153.6 km²) at aspect 2 = 17.53 km x 8.76 km plus 30 % margin each side, so |x| <= 0.8 x 17.53 km = about 1 402 000 cm = about 2^20.4, leaving about 6x headroom; about 9x at the METROPOLIS target (11.31 km x 5.66 km at aspect 2, |x| <= 905 097 cm = about 2^19.8).',['§3.2 (1)'],'kernel (population cap and 153.6 km² owned by §12.2 / §1.1)',['§12.2 cap area','§1.1 ladder'],['@rangeInvariant (justification)'],['headroom'],restates='§12.2 cap figure 153.6 km² and population cap 120 000')
it('KERNEL-0060','rule','3.2','(2) Tolerances are world lengths, never parameter-space (the OS DELTA = 1e-6 on line parameters is the anti-pattern).',['§3.2 (2)'],'kernel',[],['@insertChain','@oneEngine','@invariants'],['world-length tolerance'])
it('KERNEL-0061','constant','3.2','(2) minEdge = 25 cm: the minimum edge length on the lattice (named minEdgeCm in §2.2 LatticeSpec).',['§3.2 (2)','§2.2 LatticeSpec.minEdgeCm','§3.6','§3.10'],'§2.2 model (LatticeSpec record) / kernel (meaning)',['§2.2 LatticeSpec'],['@insertChain','@moveValidation','@invariants','@transientArena'],['minEdge','minEdgeCm'])
it('KERNEL-0062','constant','3.2','(2) attractCm = 26 cm = minEdge + 1 cm of lattice slack, so a vertex split onto an edge lies at least attractCm - 0.71 cm = 25.29 cm from both of the edge endpoints; a vertex whose exact foot lies within attractCm of an endpoint moves onto that endpoint instead (§3.6 step 2).',['§3.2 (2)','§2.2 LatticeSpec.attractCm','§3.6 step 2'],'§2.2 model (LatticeSpec record) / kernel (meaning)',['§2.2 LatticeSpec'],['@attraction','@E_KEPT','@insertChain'],['attractCm'])
it('KERNEL-0063','constant','3.2','(2) minFaceAngle = 5 deg (500 centidegrees); hard or soft by chain class (§3.10). Named minFaceAngleCdeg in §2.2 LatticeSpec.',['§3.2 (2)','§2.2 LatticeSpec.minFaceAngleCdeg','§3.6','§3.10'],'§2.2 model (LatticeSpec record) / kernel (meaning)',['§2.2 LatticeSpec'],['@E_BELOW_MIN_ANGLE','@invariants','W_ACUTE_CORNER'],['minFaceAngle','minFaceAngleCdeg'])
it('KERNEL-0064','constant','3.2','(2) Class floors come from the stage tables: minPlotWidth = 2.5 m, minInteriorAnglePlot = 12 deg (FTG sheared quads at p95 22.9 deg corner deviation stay legal - invariants police validity, not regularity), minCorridor per class.',['§3.2 (2)'],'§4.7 pipeline stage tables (plot floors)',['@plat/tables stage rows'],['@E_BELOW_MIN_WIDTH','@E_BELOW_MIN_AREA','§4.7 plots'],['minPlotWidth','minInteriorAnglePlot','minCorridor'],restates='§4.7 plot table (values)')
it('KERNEL-0065','rule','3.2','(2) Face-size floors are a minimum width and a minimum area; angle floors are angles; none of them is minEdge.',['§3.2 (2)'],'kernel',['KERNEL-0064'],['@invariants'],['face-size floors'])
it('KERNEL-0066','rule','3.2','(2) GEO-35 one minimum-edge rule for every writer: insertChain, booleans, buffers, offsets, smoothing and warp create or move edges only through §3.6 insertion semantics or move validation, and §3.10 checks the same minEdge on new AND moved edges.',['§3.2 (2)','§3.6','§3.10'],'kernel',['KERNEL-0061'],['@insertChain','@oneEngine','@warp','@invariants','§13 GEO-35 test'],['GEO-35'])
it('KERNEL-0067','rule','3.2','(2) There is no weld tolerance: coincidence is lattice-point equality, proximity is attraction (§3.6; J-K2).',['§3.2 (2)','§3.6'],'kernel',[],['@attraction'],['no weld tolerance','J-K2'])
it('KERNEL-0068','rule','3.2','(3) No float comparisons for topology: equality is index equality; "same place" is a lattice test; collinear and cocircular ties are broken deterministically on lattice positions, never on slot ids; no predicate compares a float with == 0; any float that decides a topology operation is first quantised to integer cm or to a 16-bit binary angle.',['§3.2 (3)'],'kernel',[],['@orient2d','@incircle','@insertChain','§6 determinism'],['floatTopology','16-bit binary angle','tie-break on lattice positions'])
it('KERNEL-0069','rule','3.2','(3) A distance comparison against attractCm or minEdge on a computed point (an exact foot, a crossing) uses + - x / sqrt only, so every IEEE engine gives the same answer; a borderline case may fall either way without breaking any bound, because the 1 cm between attractCm and minEdge exceeds the 0.71 cm of any single rounding.',['§3.2 (3)'],'kernel',['KERNEL-0061','KERNEL-0062'],['@attraction','§6.5 cross-platform'],['IEEE basic ops'])
it('KERNEL-0070','rule','3.2','(4) Cross-platform reproducibility is §6.5 policy (integer-derived random floats §6.1; nightly cross-platform golden run §13.3); the kernel part is integer geometry, exact predicates, one geometry engine on every host (§3.7) and a deterministic op order in every batch (§3.3).',['§3.2 (4)'],'§6.5 determinism (kernel contributes its part)',['§6.5','§6.1','§13.3'],['§13.3 nightly golden'],['cross-platform'],restates='§6.5 policy (referenced)')
it('KERNEL-0071','rule','3.2','(5) Angles and directions are unit vectors in doubles derived from integer endpoints; growth headings are stored as centidegrees.',['§3.2 (5)'],'kernel',[],['§4 growth headings (road growth)','§2.2 stored headings'],['centidegrees','growth heading'])
it('KERNEL-0072','mechanism','3.2','(6) area2(ring) (GEO-32; J-K13) returns twice the signed area as an exact integer for every ring snap can produce: each shoelace term x_i*y_(i+1) - x_(i+1)*y_i on coordinates relative to the ring bounding-box minimum corner (in [0, 2^24)), each product an exact double < 2^48; summed in a double partial flushed into a BigInt accumulator whenever its magnitude reaches 2^52 (one comparison per term), so every partial < 2^52 + 2^48 < 2^53 and every addition exact. Faces at the frame table sizes never reach the flush (terms < 2^43). Returned as an exact double because a simple ring twice-area < 2^49 (every face ring simple, §3.10).',['§3.2 (6)'],'kernel',['@rangeInvariant','@snap'],['@orientation','sliver/degeneracy decisions','area statistics in m²','@regularFit','§13 GEO-32 test'],['area2','GEO-32','J-K13','BigInt flush'])
it('KERNEL-0073','interface','3.2','(6) area2Big(ring) returns the BigInt twice-area of a self-crossing ring - only a boolean operand before its overlay can be one.',['§3.2 (6)'],'kernel',['@area2'],['@oneEngine boolean operands'],['area2Big'])
it('KERNEL-0074','receipt','3.2','(6) Receipt: three star rings of radius 2^23 - 2 whose terms all share one sign are exact (docs/validation/bench/fixall_kernel_r2_attract.out section E).',['§3.2 (6)'],'kernel',['@area2'],['§13 GEO-32 evidence'],['fixall_kernel_r2_attract.out'])
it('KERNEL-0075','rule','3.2','(6) Ring orientation is orient2d at the ring lowest-leftmost vertex; no orientation, sliver or degeneracy decision reads the sign of a float sum; area statistics in m² are derived from the exact integer.',['§3.2 (6)'],'kernel',['@orient2d'],['@sliver','@invariants','area statistics (§10, §11 export)'],['orientation','lowest-leftmost vertex'])
it('KERNEL-0076','mechanism','3.2','(7) GEO-7 the one door: snap(x, y) is the only way a number becomes an arena coordinate; in EVERY build (not only debug) it rejects !(Math.abs(v) < 2**23), one comparison per coordinate that also catches NaN and +-Infinity, with E_OUT_OF_RANGE; so NaN, +-Infinity and values past 2^31 never reach an Int32Array (which would store them silently as 0, 0 and a wrapped value - executed in GEO-7), and the null-rect crash class cannot return as a vertex at the world origin. Height and depth rasters have their own check (§2.3).',['§3.2 (7)','§3.1 E_OUT_OF_RANGE'],'kernel',['@rangeInvariant'],['@E_OUT_OF_RANGE','every coordinate producer (insertChain, booleans, skeleton, curves)','§13 GEO-7 test','§2.3 raster check (sibling)'],['snap','GEO-7','the one door'])
it('KERNEL-0077','mechanism','3.2','(8) Checked ratios: in model packages a division by a computed value goes through ratio(num, den, kind), which returns E_DEGENERATE when |den| is below the quantity floor (length 1 cm, area 1 cm², angle 1 centidegree, count 1); a lint rule flags every other division whose divisor is not a non-zero literal.',['§3.2 (8)','§3.1 dmath row'],'kernel',[],['@E_DEGENERATE','every model-package division','§13/§14 lint rule'],['ratio','quantity floor'])
it('KERNEL-0078','interface','3.2','(8) Lint rule: flags every division in model packages whose divisor is not a non-zero literal (must use ratio()).',['§3.2 (8)'],'§14 packages / §13 lint (kernel defines)',['@ratio'],['CI lint (§13/§14)'],['division lint'])
it('KERNEL-0079','mechanism','3.2','(9) Frames are rectangles (COV-VG-VN-156): a rung declares its frame AREA at its target population, A_target = frameAreaKm2AtTarget (§1.1: TOWN 4 km², CITY 25 km², METROPOLIS 64 km² = squares of 2, 5 and 8 km at the default aspect), scaled within the band by the requested population A = A_target * population / target (the side with its square root; item 9; cap figures §12.2); aspect in [0.5, 2] (default 1) gives width = sqrt(A * aspect), height = sqrt(A / aspect), centred on (0, 0) with +y north; the arena spans the frame plus marginShare on every side; every raster cols x rows follows width x height.',['§3.2 (9)','§1.1 RungProfile.frameAreaKm2AtTarget','§2.3 raster sizing'],'kernel (rung areas owned by §1.1 ladder table)',['§1.1 RungProfile.frameAreaKm2AtTarget','§1.1 RungProfile.aspect','blueprint aspect','item 9'],['@Frame','§2.2 Plat.frame','§2.3 rasters','§4.1 S1','@frameGrowth'],['frameRule','frameAreaKm2AtTarget','A_target'],restates='§1.1 rung frame areas (TOWN 4, CITY 25, METROPOLIS 64 km²)')
it('KERNEL-0080','rule','3.2','(9) The range bound of (1) is taken at the population cap and the extreme aspect, so a non-square town (village product, river and coast towns) needs no other change.',['§3.2 (9)','§3.2 frame growth'],'kernel',['@rangeInvariant'],['@frameRule','V07 village'],['extreme aspect'])
it('KERNEL-0081','rule','3.2','(9) The arena outer ring and the frame rectangle are the margin and border chains of §2.2 rule 1.',['§3.2 (9)'],'§2.2 model (chain roles)',['§2.2 rule 1'],['@frameGrowth','§4.1 S1'],['margin chain','border chain'],restates='§2.2 rule 1')
